Exactly.

17:30.480 --> 17:33.480
You cannot conjure duplication from nothing.

17:34.240 --> 17:36.200
Deletion can never create duplication.

17:36.720 --> 17:39.520
They are entirely separate, independent, structural powers.

17:39.980 --> 17:41.580
That is just deeply satisfying.

17:42.100 --> 17:44.840
It's like discovering the fundamental elements on a periodic table.

17:45.120 --> 17:46.640
You can't turn lead into gold.

17:47.380 --> 17:48.800
So, we've established our universe.

17:49.040 --> 17:50.520
We have neutral routing tools,

17:50.660 --> 17:54.040
and we have these three incredibly powerful, distinct, independent forces.

17:54.400 --> 17:58.180
The power to copy, the power to delete, and the power to reorder.

17:58.780 --> 17:59.840
Yes, the big three.

17:59.840 --> 18:06.420
And in standard logic, or standard computer programming, if you possess these powers, you are basically a god.

18:06.780 --> 18:09.860
You can use them anywhere, anytime, on any piece of data.

18:09.860 --> 18:13.020
Which brings us to the true innovation of Flyxion's work.

18:13.020 --> 18:18.760
What happens when we take these omnipotent tools and we start drawing borders around them?

18:18.760 --> 18:19.400
Right.

18:19.400 --> 18:21.560
This is the core twist.

18:22.300 --> 18:24.820
What if we introduce strict locations?

18:25.640 --> 18:29.360
Picture yourself walking into a massive, sprawling corporate office building.

18:29.860 --> 18:31.020
You are an executive.

18:31.420 --> 18:32.220
I like this analogy.

18:32.220 --> 18:35.760
In your pocket, you carry three ultimate administrative tools.

18:35.760 --> 18:38.480
A flash drive that can copy any file, which is W.

18:38.840 --> 18:41.440
A shredder that can destroy any document, K.

18:41.840 --> 18:46.220
And a sorting machine that can reorder any filing cabinet, C.

18:46.800 --> 18:51.800
If you are standing in the main lobby, what the paper refers to as the root level of the formula,

18:51.800 --> 18:53.120
you're a super user.

18:53.680 --> 18:57.180
The rules of the lobby say you are allowed to use all three of your tools.

18:57.880 --> 19:00.300
But formulas, like office buildings, have structure.

19:00.760 --> 19:02.000
They have nested components.

19:02.700 --> 19:08.200
The paper visualizes these separate spaces as brackets surrounding a piece of the formula.

19:08.360 --> 19:08.960
Okay, brackets.

19:08.960 --> 19:13.220
And on the outside of each bracket is a little subscript label, like an L.

19:13.900 --> 19:19.360
So mathematically, it looks like a bracket, some data inside, a closing bracket, and the letter L.

19:19.640 --> 19:24.340
So from the main lobby, I open a door and walk into a specific office, room L.

19:24.920 --> 19:27.060
Now, room L has a sign on the door.

19:27.620 --> 19:29.400
It's a localized permission structure.

19:29.960 --> 19:31.560
And the sign on the door says,

19:31.560 --> 19:37.520
In this room, you are only allowed to use the shredder, K, and the sorting machine, C.

19:38.160 --> 19:42.540
Copying files, W, is strictly prohibited by corporate policy.

19:42.980 --> 19:43.460
Exactly.

19:44.000 --> 19:49.820
The label L dictates precisely which of those three structural powers are permitted inside that specific bracket.

19:50.100 --> 19:50.360
Okay.

19:50.500 --> 19:53.260
So let's say you have a piece of code, a reducible expression,

19:53.700 --> 19:55.760
that is trying to execute a copy-paste command.

19:56.220 --> 19:59.540
If that code is sitting out in the lobby, it executes perfectly.

19:59.540 --> 20:05.940
But if that exact same piece of code is located inside bracket L, the copy-paste step is blocked.

20:05.940 --> 20:13.440
The paper is incredibly careful here to distinguish between two concepts, availability and admissibility.

20:14.480 --> 20:16.600
Availability versus admissibility.

20:17.120 --> 20:20.060
This tripped me up at first, but it is such a vital distinction.

20:20.260 --> 20:24.500
I want you to break that down for the listener because understanding this is the key to the whole paper.

20:24.500 --> 20:26.400
Let's use your office analogy.

20:26.900 --> 20:27.720
You walk into room L.

20:28.260 --> 20:30.400
You still have the W flash drive in your pocket.

20:30.920 --> 20:32.620
The tool hasn't vanished from the universe.

20:33.000 --> 20:36.820
The system as a whole still recognizes what a copy-paste command is.

20:37.220 --> 20:38.220
The tool is available.

20:38.540 --> 20:38.760
Right.

20:38.820 --> 20:39.800
I physically possess it.

20:40.000 --> 20:46.260
But because you are standing inside room L, the local rules forbid its use.

20:46.260 --> 20:52.340
Therefore, executing that copy-paste command is no longer admissible at your specific location.

20:53.040 --> 20:55.900
Availability is about what exists in the system globally.

20:56.940 --> 21:00.580
Admissibility is about what is legally allowed to happen locally.

21:00.980 --> 21:03.300
Wait, let's play this out practically.

21:03.760 --> 21:04.000
Okay.

21:04.240 --> 21:10.380
Because if you've ever tried to debug code, you know that when a program tries to do something it doesn't have permission to do, it usually crashes.

21:10.620 --> 21:11.160
Sure, yeah.

21:11.160 --> 21:16.240
It throws a fatal error or a segmentation fault and the whole program halts and catches fire.

21:16.720 --> 21:25.120
So if I have a formula sitting inside room L, and the formula says, execute the W command, but the room forbids W, what happens?

21:26.020 --> 21:28.480
Does the math equation just crash?

21:28.720 --> 21:29.900
No, it doesn't crash at all.

21:30.440 --> 21:31.340
It just freezes.

21:31.940 --> 21:35.540
It becomes what computer scientists call a normal form.

21:36.160 --> 21:37.040
A normal form.

21:37.660 --> 21:39.020
But it hasn't finished calculating.

21:39.020 --> 21:42.020
There's literally a command sitting right there waiting to be executed.

21:42.640 --> 21:45.200
Ah, but finished is a relative term.

21:45.860 --> 21:53.340
In mathematics, a term is considered to be in a normal form when there are absolutely no admissible steps left for it to take.

21:53.760 --> 21:55.180
Oh, admissible steps.

21:55.340 --> 21:55.520
Right.

21:56.320 --> 22:02.220
Usually that means the calculation has completely resolved down to its final answer, like 2 plus 2 becoming 4.

22:02.220 --> 22:13.860
But in Flyxion's scoped universe, a term can also reach a normal form simply because the laws of physics in that specific room prevent it from moving any further.

22:14.600 --> 22:16.000
That's wild.

22:16.000 --> 22:29.900
To use an example from the paper, if the delete key, K, is allowed in the main lobby but forbidden inside room L, then a formula trying to execute a delete command inside room L becomes completely inert.

22:29.900 --> 22:36.500
It sits there, frozen in time, containing an available tool that is permanently inadmissible.

22:36.960 --> 22:39.100
That is so eerie to think about.

22:39.740 --> 22:48.180
It's like holding a loaded spring in your hand, but the fundamental laws of thermodynamics in that one specific room prevent the spring from ever releasing.

22:48.820 --> 22:51.720
It's just stored potential energy that can never be realized.

22:52.080 --> 22:52.300
Yes.

22:52.300 --> 22:55.880
Okay, but what if the architecture of the building is more complicated?

22:56.460 --> 22:59.000
What if there are rooms within rooms within rooms?

22:59.120 --> 23:05.020
Like, I walk from the lobby into room L, and then inside room L there is a locked closet.

23:05.160 --> 23:06.020
Let's call it room M.

23:06.500 --> 23:11.100
This is where the paper introduces the calculation of effective permissions.

23:11.840 --> 23:19.160
To figure out what you are allowed to do at any specific spot deep inside a formula, you have to calculate the effective permission.

23:19.160 --> 23:27.200
And you do this by taking the intersection of the permissions of every single bracket that encloses you, all the way back out to the root level.

23:27.580 --> 23:28.480
The intersection.

23:29.080 --> 23:36.200
For those who might be a bit rusty on set theory, intersection means you only keep the items that appear in every single list.

23:36.380 --> 23:42.340
You only keep the permissions that are explicitly allowed by every single door you had to walk through to get to where you are.

23:42.720 --> 23:43.200
Precisely.

23:43.780 --> 23:47.620
And this leads to a very important lemma in the paper, Lemma 3.5.

23:47.620 --> 23:54.800
It states that effective permissions can only ever shrink, they can never grow, as you move deeper into nested brackets.

23:55.740 --> 23:57.500
Let's map it onto your office building.

23:58.020 --> 23:59.320
Okay, let's trace my walk.

23:59.860 --> 24:00.740
I start in the main lobby.

24:01.140 --> 24:02.560
The lobby is the root.

24:02.840 --> 24:03.820
It allows everything.

24:04.020 --> 24:05.360
Copy, delete, and reorder.

24:05.860 --> 24:06.940
You step into room L.

24:07.660 --> 24:11.620
The sign on door L says it allows delete and reorder, but forbids copy.

24:12.080 --> 24:13.200
So you lose W.

24:13.200 --> 24:17.240
Your effective permission is now just K, C.

24:17.840 --> 24:18.120
Right.

24:18.640 --> 24:23.280
Now, while standing inside room L, I see the door to closet M.

24:23.860 --> 24:24.860
I walk into the closet.

24:25.460 --> 24:29.080
Now, let's say the sign on closet M is actually very permissive.

24:29.220 --> 24:34.500
The sign on the closet door says, I allow copy W and delete K.

24:34.880 --> 24:35.660
What happens?

24:35.820 --> 24:37.220
Do I get my copy power back?

24:37.720 --> 24:38.740
No, you do not.

24:39.400 --> 24:46.280
Because to calculate your effective permission in the closet, you have to intersect the closet's rules with the rules of the room you were already in.

24:46.920 --> 24:50.280
Ah, because I'm still physically inside room L.

24:50.580 --> 24:51.040
Exactly.

24:51.040 --> 24:57.820
The closet allows W and K, but room L, which entirely surrounds the closet, already stripped away your W power.

24:58.300 --> 25:01.780
You cannot exercise a power in the closet that was forbidden by the outer room.

25:02.320 --> 25:09.140
So the intersection of K, C and W, K leaves you with only one effective permission, K.

25:09.660 --> 25:11.420
In the closet, you can only delete.

25:11.920 --> 25:12.140
Wow.

25:12.700 --> 25:18.260
A nested scope can further restrict what its ancestors allow, but it can never, ever extend it.

25:18.260 --> 25:23.000
But you cannot regain a lost privilege simply by walking deeper into a restricted area.

25:23.760 --> 25:25.420
And there was a profound consequence to this.

25:25.880 --> 25:28.980
Let's say a piece of code is frozen because it lacked a permission.

25:29.160 --> 25:32.620
It became that eerie, inert, normal form we talked about.

25:33.580 --> 25:40.680
Because permissions only shrink as you go deeper, if you take that frozen piece of code and bury it even deeper into more nested brackets,

25:41.060 --> 25:44.540
it will never spontaneously start calculating again.

25:45.000 --> 25:48.020
Moving it inward can only take away more permissions.

25:48.260 --> 25:50.440
It can never grant the missing one.

25:50.980 --> 25:53.920
Once it's stuck, moving it deeper keeps it stuck.

25:54.660 --> 25:54.880
Okay.

25:55.020 --> 25:59.100
If everything just stayed in its own room, the system would be incredibly simple.

25:59.480 --> 26:04.500
You just look at where a piece of data is, calculate the effective permissions of that specific room,

26:04.980 --> 26:08.140
run whatever commands are allowed, and freeze whatever commands aren't.

26:08.260 --> 26:09.700
It's entirely static.

26:10.080 --> 26:10.940
It would be, yes.

26:10.940 --> 26:13.480
But computer programs aren't static.

26:13.800 --> 26:14.860
Logic isn't static.

26:15.020 --> 26:16.080
Things have to interact.

26:16.260 --> 26:18.440
Functions need to pull data toward them.

26:18.880 --> 26:23.040
A formula in one room needs to grab a variable from another room to complete its calculation.

26:23.560 --> 26:26.920
What happens when these terms start crossing borders?

26:26.920 --> 26:30.260
And here we reach the absolute heart of the conflict in this paper.

26:30.840 --> 26:32.900
The threat to something called confluence.

26:33.500 --> 26:34.100
Confluence.

26:34.380 --> 26:41.860
But before we examine how confluence is broken, we have to understand why confluence is the holy grail of computer science.

26:42.360 --> 26:45.760
We have to define the piece before we introduce the threat.

26:45.760 --> 26:46.160
Confluence.

26:46.160 --> 26:46.760
Right.

26:47.580 --> 26:48.180
Confluence.

26:48.600 --> 26:55.820
If you've ever taken a math class and solved a long, complex algebraic equation, you intuitively understand confluence.

26:56.200 --> 27:00.660
It's the comforting guarantee that no matter what order you do your calculations in,

27:01.140 --> 27:06.040
as long as you follow the basic rules of math, you will eventually arrive at the exact same final answer.

27:06.780 --> 27:11.360
Let's say I have the equation parentheses 2 plus 3, parentheses 4 plus 1.

27:11.700 --> 27:12.540
You have choices there.

27:12.760 --> 27:13.100
Exactly.

27:13.100 --> 27:20.980
I could choose to add 2 plus 3 first, get 5, and then add 4 plus 1 to get 5, and then multiply them to get 25.

27:21.500 --> 27:24.600
Or I could choose to add 4 plus 1 first.

27:25.260 --> 27:30.000
It doesn't matter which path I take or which side of the equation I evaluate first.

27:30.160 --> 27:32.740
I am guaranteed to reach 25.

27:33.820 --> 27:37.020
The paths diverge, but they eventually flow back together.

27:37.420 --> 27:40.440
They are confluent, like rivers joining together.

27:40.440 --> 27:44.240
And in computing, confluence is essential for predictability.

27:44.940 --> 27:51.420
If a system is confluent, it means that while the computer might have thousands of different valid choices of what to process next,

27:51.940 --> 27:54.160
those choices can always be reconciled.

27:54.700 --> 27:58.240
All valid paths lead to the same final normal form.

27:58.500 --> 27:58.740
Right.

27:58.740 --> 28:03.140
And early in the paper, theorem 4.1 proves a comforting fact.

28:03.140 --> 28:09.120
If we keep all our structural tools locked in their static rooms and never let anything cross a boundary,

28:09.400 --> 28:12.260
the system is perfectly mathematically confluent.

28:12.640 --> 28:13.560
It is safe.

28:13.880 --> 28:15.700
But like we said, things have to move.

28:16.340 --> 28:19.460
So, Flyxion introduces a boundary rule to allow movement,

28:19.460 --> 28:23.380
specifically a rule called ABS, which stands for absorption.

28:23.880 --> 28:26.700
And this is the rule that shatters the universe.

28:27.100 --> 28:27.660
It really does.

28:28.860 --> 28:34.300
The rule of absorption is a mechanism for a function inside a restricted room to reach out and pull data inside.

28:35.260 --> 28:36.740
Mathematically, it is written like this.

28:37.540 --> 28:40.500
If you have a function U sitting inside bracket L,

28:41.200 --> 28:45.120
and it is being applied to some data V that is sitting outside the bracket,

28:45.400 --> 28:47.480
the function can absorb the data.

28:48.020 --> 28:48.800
Pulling it in.

28:48.800 --> 28:53.680
The boundary expands to swallow V, bringing it inside room L alongside U.

28:54.120 --> 28:57.960
Okay, I want everyone listening to pause and really visualize this,

28:58.320 --> 29:01.820
because this is where the paper stops being just abstract math

29:01.820 --> 29:05.060
and starts mirroring real-world security systems.

29:05.480 --> 29:08.800
I want you to imagine pulling something through an airport security checkpoint.

29:09.040 --> 29:10.040
I love this analogy.

29:10.660 --> 29:14.600
Let's say I am in the main terminal of the airport, the root lobby.

29:15.100 --> 29:17.480
The rules of the main terminal are very permissive.

29:17.480 --> 29:18.940
I have a full water bottle.

29:19.300 --> 29:21.240
The main terminal allows liquids.

29:21.640 --> 29:25.020
So if I evaluate my current state before I go through security,

29:25.300 --> 29:28.200
I can execute the drink water command.

29:28.560 --> 29:29.740
The action is admissible.

29:30.540 --> 29:31.540
Right, because you're in the lobby.

29:32.220 --> 29:36.360
But let's say I choose to use the absorption rule first.

29:36.360 --> 29:42.000
I take my water bottle, and I walk through the TSA security checkpoint and into the gate area.

29:42.400 --> 29:44.600
The gate area is a restricted scope.

29:45.120 --> 29:46.820
It explicitly forbids liquids.

29:47.180 --> 29:52.300
If I pull my water bottle across that boundary first, the TSA agent confiscates it.

29:52.740 --> 29:55.380
The drink water command is now permanently frozen.

29:55.380 --> 30:01.000
I get a totally fundamentally different result in my life purely based on the order of operations.

30:01.320 --> 30:05.460
That is a phenomenal, visceral way to understand theorem 4.2.

30:05.800 --> 30:09.900
That theorem formally states the moment you introduce the absorption rule,

30:10.460 --> 30:12.600
you ruin confluence completely.

30:13.200 --> 30:13.540
Wow.

30:13.540 --> 30:19.960
The very act of pulling data across a boundary changes the fundamental laws of physics governing that data.

30:20.760 --> 30:25.240
A step that was perfectly legal a moment ago becomes illegal just because its zip code changed.

30:25.440 --> 30:29.600
The paper provides a very specific mathematical counterexample to prove this,

30:29.700 --> 30:30.600
and I want to trace it out,

30:30.740 --> 30:35.160
because it proves that the uniqueness of final answers is utterly destroyed.

30:35.540 --> 30:36.620
Let's set the stage.

30:36.620 --> 30:41.280
We have our root lobby, and we have a restricted room called Scope L.

30:41.700 --> 30:47.720
The lobby permits everything, including the delete key, K, but Scope L strictly forbids K.

30:48.240 --> 30:50.580
So we construct a formula that looks like this.

30:51.100 --> 30:55.280
We have a function, let's call it A, sitting inside the restricted room L.

30:55.780 --> 30:57.240
So A is inside the brackets.

30:57.900 --> 31:01.600
Right outside the door, waiting in the lobby, is a package of data.

31:01.600 --> 31:06.700
And that package of data is a reducible expression, K, B, C.

31:07.320 --> 31:08.620
Let me visualize that clearly.

31:09.440 --> 31:11.040
Inside room L, we have A.

31:11.800 --> 31:14.980
Outside room L, we have parentheses K, B, C.

31:15.700 --> 31:18.520
Now, K, B, C is a command waiting to happen.

31:18.740 --> 31:22.040
It wants to use the delete key on C, leaving only B.

31:22.600 --> 31:22.980
Exactly.

31:23.580 --> 31:25.820
Now, you are the computer processor.

31:26.320 --> 31:28.940
You look at this setup, and you realize you have a choice.

31:28.940 --> 31:33.980
There are two completely valid legal moves you could make right now.

31:34.400 --> 31:36.360
We will call them path 1 and path 2.

31:37.080 --> 31:38.100
Let's walk down path 1.

31:38.720 --> 31:43.620
Path 1 is evaluating the package before taking it through the airport security checkpoint.

31:44.360 --> 31:45.940
So I look at parentheses K, B, C.

31:46.500 --> 31:47.840
It's sitting out in the root lobby.

31:48.580 --> 31:51.760
The lobby allows the delete key, so I execute the command.

31:52.500 --> 31:56.820
Parentheses K, B, C, deletes the C and reduces down to just a simple B.

31:57.460 --> 31:58.220
Perfectly valid.

31:58.220 --> 32:01.640
So now, your entire overall formula has simplified.

32:02.360 --> 32:04.840
You still have A sitting inside room L.

32:05.300 --> 32:09.000
But now, waiting outside the door is just the simple data B.

32:09.380 --> 32:12.220
And now, A looks out the door and says, come here, B.

32:12.500 --> 32:14.660
It executes the absorption rule.

32:15.160 --> 32:18.500
The boundary of room L expands and pulls B inside.

32:19.160 --> 32:23.160
So now, both A and B are sitting safely inside room L.

32:23.500 --> 32:25.740
They aren't trying to execute any forbidden commands.

32:26.020 --> 32:27.020
They are just sitting there.

32:27.020 --> 32:30.660
We have reached our final answer, our normal form.

32:31.240 --> 32:34.860
Path 1 results in the package A, B, inside room L.

32:35.580 --> 32:36.520
Flawless execution.

32:37.340 --> 32:39.120
Now, let's rewind time.

32:39.620 --> 32:40.880
We go back to the starting state.

32:40.880 --> 32:43.300
We have A inside room L.

32:43.300 --> 32:49.120
And outside in the lobby, we have the unevaluated package, paren, K, comma, B, close paren.

32:49.120 --> 32:52.080
This time, you choose path 2.

32:52.080 --> 32:56.960
You decide to perform the absorption step first before evaluating the package.

32:56.960 --> 33:07.440
Okay, so A reaches out of its room, grabs the entire raw expression, paren, K, comma, B, comma, C, and drags it through the security checkpoint into room L.

33:07.440 --> 33:14.420
So now I have a massive package sitting inside room L, A, combined with paren, K, comma, B, comma, C.

33:14.420 --> 33:16.580
And now what does the system try to do?

33:16.580 --> 33:22.680
Well, the system looks at K, bracket, B, C, and says, okay, time to execute the delete command.

33:23.340 --> 33:23.780
But wait.

33:24.300 --> 33:27.420
It looks at the sign on the door of the room it is currently standing in.

33:27.720 --> 33:30.740
Room L explicitly forbids the delete key, K.

33:30.740 --> 33:33.320
The TSA just confiscated the tool.

33:34.040 --> 33:35.360
The step is no longer admissible.

33:35.940 --> 33:37.220
So the formula just stops.

33:37.380 --> 33:38.120
It freezes.

33:38.520 --> 33:40.620
It becomes a normal form right there.

33:41.580 --> 33:41.960
Exactly.

33:42.600 --> 33:50.320
Your final answer on path 2 is the package A, bracket, K, B, C, permanently frozen inside room L.

33:51.220 --> 33:52.420
But look at what just happened.

33:53.140 --> 33:56.140
Path 1 gave us A, paren, bracket, B inside the room.

33:56.640 --> 34:00.920
Path 2 gave us A, paren, bracket, K, B, C inside the room.

34:01.240 --> 34:04.280
Those are two completely different, irreconcilable realities.

34:04.900 --> 34:05.700
The math is broken.

34:06.520 --> 34:11.160
Just by changing the arbitrary order in which the computer decided to process the steps,

34:11.500 --> 34:14.100
we arrived at different physical outcomes.

34:14.660 --> 34:17.240
This is the crux of the crisis in the paper.

34:17.980 --> 34:24.760
As Flyxion writes, a boundary rule can move a redex from a position where it is permitted to one where it is not.

34:24.760 --> 34:31.460
The legality of a mathematical transformation is now heavily dependent on its geographic position in the syntax tree.

34:31.900 --> 34:32.020
Right.

34:32.980 --> 34:40.020
If we want to build a system that is predictable, a system that doesn't just randomly generate different answers based on background processing speeds,

34:40.660 --> 34:43.840
we have to restrict when a piece of data is allowed to cross a boundary.

34:44.320 --> 34:47.000
We have to restore order to this universe.

34:47.360 --> 34:50.760
Which brings us to the next massive phase of the paper, restoring order.

34:50.760 --> 34:56.720
If the fundamental problem is that pulling a piece of data across a border changes how it evaluates,

34:57.080 --> 35:04.160
the incredibly obvious common sense solution seems to be just make sure it is completely done evaluating before you pull it across.

35:04.580 --> 35:08.920
Make sure it has reached its final answer out in the lobby and then bring it inside.

35:09.540 --> 35:12.340
That is the intuitive leap almost any computer scientist would make.

35:12.760 --> 35:14.100
You would propose a safety protocol.

35:14.540 --> 35:18.060
Only absorb a piece of data if that data is R-normal.

35:18.440 --> 35:19.100
R-normal.

35:19.100 --> 35:25.420
Right. R-normal is just a technical way of saying the data has no permitted steps left to take at its current location.

35:25.740 --> 35:26.860
It's done cooking.

35:27.760 --> 35:34.020
But theorem 4.4 in the paper proves that even this seemingly airtight safety rule is fundamentally flawed.

35:34.340 --> 35:35.220
It is not enough.

35:35.680 --> 35:37.580
Wait, I am genuinely lost here.

35:37.580 --> 35:43.520
If a piece of data is totally done evaluating, if it literally has no more moves it can make,

35:43.900 --> 35:46.640
how can moving it into a new room change anything?

35:46.760 --> 35:49.160
It has no moves left to change.

35:49.580 --> 35:57.720
Because of a deeply insidious concept called appending absorption that can be hidden like a Trojan horse inside the data itself.

35:57.720 --> 35:58.720
Oh no.

35:59.040 --> 36:02.900
The paper walks through a truly mind-bending counterexample to prove this.

36:03.440 --> 36:06.380
It demonstrates that a package of data can look completely finished.

36:06.840 --> 36:10.480
It can have absolutely zero permitted steps available in its current state.

36:10.860 --> 36:12.660
But it secretly contains a trap.

36:13.340 --> 36:16.420
And the mere act of crossing a boundary springs that trap.

36:16.420 --> 36:22.040
Okay, we have to dissect this trap slowly because this is where the paper reads like a mathematical heist movie.

36:22.400 --> 36:25.980
How do you build a Trojan horse in combinatory logic?

36:26.840 --> 36:29.680
We need to set up a slightly more complex building this time.

36:30.260 --> 36:31.940
We need two restricted rooms.

36:32.420 --> 36:35.180
Let's call them the sigma room and the tau room.

36:35.300 --> 36:36.020
Sigma and tau?

36:36.500 --> 36:39.360
And we need a very special structural tool.

36:39.860 --> 36:41.340
Let's invent a tool called P.

36:42.080 --> 36:43.960
P is a specialized delete key.

36:43.960 --> 36:47.940
It takes two arguments and it just deletes the second one.

36:48.700 --> 36:49.860
But here is the catch.

36:50.740 --> 36:52.620
P is a highly controversial tool.

36:53.380 --> 36:57.280
It is permitted everywhere in the building except inside the tau room.

36:57.960 --> 37:00.100
The tau room is like the ultimate VIP lounge.

37:00.520 --> 37:02.600
And the P tool is strictly banned from entering.

37:03.500 --> 37:08.740
If the label tau is anywhere in your chain of permissions, the P tool is instantly frozen.

37:09.160 --> 37:10.400
Okay, I am picturing it.

37:10.400 --> 37:18.320
P is a VIP pass that works in the lobby, it works in the sigma room, it works everywhere, except it is blacklisted from the tau room.

37:18.580 --> 37:18.940
Got it.

37:19.620 --> 37:21.320
Now we construct our starting formula.

37:21.960 --> 37:26.860
On the left side, sitting inside the exclusive tau room, we have a function called C.

37:27.600 --> 37:30.520
C is looking out the door, waiting to absorb some data.

37:31.240 --> 37:31.500
Okay.

37:32.100 --> 37:33.480
C is inside tau.

37:33.480 --> 37:38.080
On the right side, out in the lobby, is the data package that C wants to absorb.

37:38.880 --> 37:40.100
But this package is complicated.

37:40.900 --> 37:42.260
Let's call the whole package V.

37:43.260 --> 37:46.940
Inside package V, there is a mini room, the sigma room.

37:47.920 --> 37:53.520
Inside the sigma room is our controversial tool, P, and a single piece of data, A.

37:53.520 --> 38:00.260
Right outside the sigma room, still inside the overall package V, is a second piece of data, B.

38:01.140 --> 38:04.060
Let me make sure I have this spatial arrangement perfect.

38:04.680 --> 38:06.560
Package V is floating in the lobby.

38:07.360 --> 38:11.320
Inside V, there is a sigma room holding P and A.

38:11.960 --> 38:14.680
And sitting right next to that sigma room is B.

38:15.300 --> 38:15.760
Exactly.

38:16.440 --> 38:19.020
Now, let's test your intuitive safety rule.

38:19.740 --> 38:21.940
Look at package V sitting out in the lobby.

38:21.940 --> 38:24.860
Is V currently our normal?

38:25.560 --> 38:27.820
Does it have any permitted steps it can take right now?

38:28.660 --> 38:29.900
Let's look inside it.

38:30.560 --> 38:33.140
Inside the sigma room, we have P and A.

38:33.660 --> 38:37.640
Now, you said the P tool requires two arguments to fire.

38:38.020 --> 38:41.000
It needs two pieces of data so it can delete the second one.

38:41.560 --> 38:42.860
Right now, it only has A.

38:43.320 --> 38:45.140
It's a partial application.

38:45.520 --> 38:47.620
It's like a machine waiting for its second battery.

38:48.060 --> 38:49.640
So, P cannot fire.

38:50.300 --> 38:50.600
Correct.

38:50.600 --> 38:54.520
And B is just sitting outside the sigma room doing nothing.

38:54.780 --> 38:55.580
So, no.

38:55.760 --> 38:57.580
Nothing in package V can evaluate.

38:58.060 --> 38:59.180
It is completely stuck.

38:59.400 --> 39:00.520
It looks utterly dormant.

39:00.760 --> 39:01.980
It is our normal.

39:02.780 --> 39:03.260
Precisely.

39:03.800 --> 39:08.380
So, under your proposed safety rule, only absorb our normal arguments.

39:08.920 --> 39:14.180
The function C, sitting inside the towel room, looks at package V, sees that it is dormant, and says,

39:14.640 --> 39:15.680
You are safe to enter.

39:15.680 --> 39:20.300
C reaches out and pulls the entire package V into the towel room.

39:20.300 --> 39:22.820
Okay, so C drags the whole thing inside.

39:23.180 --> 39:28.340
Now, we have a massive formula where absolutely everything is enclosed inside the VIP towel room.

39:28.340 --> 39:33.380
Now, look incredibly closely at what is happening inside the towel room right now.

39:34.060 --> 39:36.300
You have C applied to package V.

39:37.040 --> 39:38.840
But, what was package V?

39:39.540 --> 39:40.280
Let's see.

39:40.420 --> 39:42.040
It was a sigma room.

39:42.540 --> 39:43.000
Exactly.

39:43.320 --> 39:48.720
It was a sigma room containing a function P, A, sitting right next to a piece of data, B.

39:49.500 --> 39:52.840
This is exactly the geometric setup for another absorption.

39:53.760 --> 39:54.800
Oh, my God.

39:55.300 --> 39:57.840
The sigma room is going to reach out and absorb B.

39:58.400 --> 39:59.500
That's the pending absorption.

39:59.900 --> 40:02.400
It was a boundary rule waiting to happen inside the package itself.

40:02.840 --> 40:05.140
Okay, so B gets sucked into the sigma room.

40:05.280 --> 40:10.080
Now, we have C sitting next to a sigma room that contains P, A, and B.

40:10.600 --> 40:12.320
And now look at what we have created.

40:12.320 --> 40:19.200
Inside that sigma room, the controversial tool P finally has its two arguments, A and B.

40:19.880 --> 40:21.000
The redex is complete.

40:21.540 --> 40:22.880
The second battery is inserted.

40:23.400 --> 40:24.540
The spring is loaded.

40:25.340 --> 40:26.940
P, A, B wants to fire.

40:27.560 --> 40:29.740
Delete B and reduce to A.

40:30.520 --> 40:30.880
But wait.

40:31.520 --> 40:32.420
Look at where it's sitting.

40:33.040 --> 40:35.060
It is inside the sigma room, yes.

40:35.440 --> 40:36.680
But where is the sigma room?

40:37.060 --> 40:40.500
The sigma room is now sitting entirely inside the towel room.

40:40.500 --> 40:43.920
We pulled the whole package into the VIP lounge in the very first step.

40:44.760 --> 40:49.460
And our strict rule was that the P tool is blacklisted if tau is anywhere in the chain above it.

40:49.900 --> 40:50.340
Exactly.

40:50.720 --> 40:54.340
The effective permission of that spot is the intersection of tau and sigma.

40:54.920 --> 40:58.000
And because tau forbids P, the step is blocked.

40:58.880 --> 40:59.960
The tool is confiscated.

41:00.060 --> 41:01.940
The term is permanently frozen.

41:02.740 --> 41:05.920
The trap is sprung, but the environment prevents it from resolving.

41:05.920 --> 41:11.680
Your final answer on this path is a permanently jammed mechanism inside the towel room.

41:12.060 --> 41:12.540
Okay.

41:13.040 --> 41:16.100
But what if we run it the other way?

41:16.660 --> 41:19.600
What if we don't absorb the package into the towel room immediately?

41:20.040 --> 41:21.660
Well, let's rewind to the start.

41:22.280 --> 41:24.440
The package V is sitting out in the lobby.

41:24.860 --> 41:27.040
We know it has a pending absorption inside it.

41:27.500 --> 41:31.360
Let's execute that pending absorption first out in the lobby.

41:32.100 --> 41:32.320
Okay.

41:33.020 --> 41:36.920
So out in the free lobby, the sigma room reaches out and absorbs B.

41:38.000 --> 41:41.260
Now the sigma room contains P, A, and B.

41:41.980 --> 41:43.320
P has both its batteries.

41:43.820 --> 41:44.680
It is ready to fire.

41:45.500 --> 41:46.900
It checks its permissions.

41:47.600 --> 41:49.240
It is inside the sigma room.

41:49.740 --> 41:51.860
Is the restrictive towel room anywhere above it?

41:52.420 --> 41:52.800
No.

41:53.140 --> 41:54.200
So it's out in the root lobby.

41:55.160 --> 41:56.940
So P is permitted.

41:57.580 --> 41:58.240
It fires.

41:58.820 --> 41:59.720
P deletes B.

41:59.720 --> 42:02.860
And the whole thing reduces down to just A.

42:03.520 --> 42:11.380
So now our entire massive package V has reduced down to a single piece of data, A, sitting inside a sigma room.

42:11.680 --> 42:17.160
And now finally, the C function, sitting in the VIP towel room, reaches out and absorbs it.

42:17.280 --> 42:23.320
So we end up with C and the sigma room holding A all inside the towel room.

42:23.740 --> 42:24.080
Yes.

42:24.080 --> 42:27.420
And I want you to compare that to our previous result.

42:28.120 --> 42:31.580
The first path gave us a jammed P machine holding two pieces of data.

42:31.780 --> 42:34.520
The second path gave us a clean, fully resolved A.

42:34.680 --> 42:36.440
They are completely different realities.

42:36.440 --> 42:40.020
This is the sheer brilliance of theorem 4.4.

42:40.280 --> 42:45.140
It proves mathematically that merely waiting for an argument to look done isn't enough.

42:45.540 --> 42:48.480
Because an argument can harbor a structural trap.

42:48.900 --> 42:49.280
Wow.

42:49.280 --> 42:56.120
Moving it into a restricted scope changes the rules of the environment before that hidden red X is exposed.

42:56.860 --> 42:59.920
By the time the trap springs, the permissions have changed.

43:00.280 --> 43:03.200
And the formula tries to evaluate under the wrong set of laws.

43:03.660 --> 43:07.060
It's like sneaking a dormant computer virus through a firewall.

43:07.440 --> 43:11.560
And then it activates on the other side where the antivirus software isn't looking for it.

43:12.060 --> 43:14.500
So how on earth do we fix this?

43:14.500 --> 43:19.800
We can't have a computational logic system that just shatters if a function absorbs an argument in the wrong order.

43:20.560 --> 43:25.160
The paper provides two distinct mathematically robust safety protocols to restore confluence.

43:25.700 --> 43:30.820
The first is outlined in theorem 4.7, the concept of settled arguments.

43:31.320 --> 43:32.180
Settled arguments.

43:32.500 --> 43:35.780
How does that differ from just being our normal and looking done?

43:35.780 --> 43:42.000
An argument is considered settled if it is our normal, meaning no actual combinators can fire right now.

43:42.220 --> 43:48.220
And it has absolutely zero pending absorptions left anywhere inside its structure.

43:49.040 --> 43:49.520
No traps.

43:50.020 --> 43:50.340
Exactly.

43:50.900 --> 43:52.820
You cannot just glance at the surface.

43:52.960 --> 43:57.240
You have to recursively scan the entire nested architecture of the package.

43:57.740 --> 44:04.200
If there is even one internal bracket sitting next to a variable waiting to pull it in, the package is not settled.

44:04.200 --> 44:08.440
You are strictly forbidden from moving the overarching package across a border.

44:08.820 --> 44:13.720
You must wait for all the internal dust to settle everywhere before you transfer it.

44:14.340 --> 44:15.120
I really like that.

44:15.720 --> 44:17.820
It's a highly conservative safety protocol.

44:18.200 --> 44:22.620
Wait until absolutely every internal process is finished before you go through security.

44:23.060 --> 44:25.560
But the paper offers a second solution, right?

44:25.880 --> 44:28.320
Theorem 4.9, insensitive arguments?

44:28.800 --> 44:29.200
Yes.

44:29.860 --> 44:32.680
The settled condition is safe, but it's very restrictive.

44:32.680 --> 44:37.100
The counter example we just mapped out needed two things to cause a paradox.

44:37.840 --> 44:46.120
It needed a pending absorption, the trapdoor, and it needed a controversial tool, the p-combinator, that was sensitive to the room's rules.

44:46.840 --> 44:49.020
Theorem 4.9 asks a clever question.

44:49.520 --> 44:55.100
What if we allow the trapdoors to exist as long as the package doesn't contain any controversial tools?

44:55.100 --> 44:57.940
Ah. Let's go back to section one.

44:58.360 --> 44:59.960
We split the tools into two groups.

45:00.280 --> 45:02.300
The structural ones, copy, delete, reorder.

45:02.580 --> 45:04.020
Those are the controversial ones.

45:04.500 --> 45:06.660
Those are the ones that get banned by room permissions.

45:07.300 --> 45:13.200
But the neutral tools, identity I and composition B, they just route traffic.

45:13.420 --> 45:13.780
Exactly.

45:13.780 --> 45:15.460
They are entirely harmless.

45:15.620 --> 45:17.940
They are permitted in every single room, universally.

45:18.340 --> 45:18.860
Precisely.

45:19.440 --> 45:30.800
If a package of data is insensitive, meaning you scan it and confirm it is built exclusively from INB and contains absolutely zero structural combinators, then it literally does not matter what room editors.

45:31.920 --> 45:33.160
Because they're always legal.

45:33.380 --> 45:33.600
Right.

45:33.600 --> 45:39.840
The rules governing its internal evaluation will never be revoked, because its tools are universally legal.

45:40.400 --> 45:45.920
Therefore, even if it has a pending absorption hidden inside it, you can safely drag it across a boundary.

45:46.580 --> 45:54.480
When that pending absorption finally fires on the other side, it won't suddenly find its permissions revoked, because it's only using universally approved tools.

45:54.480 --> 46:05.260
So if my water bottle is officially approved by every single aviation security agency on planet Earth, it doesn't matter if I decide to drink it in the lobby or at the gate, the result is perfectly identical.

46:06.540 --> 46:08.240
That is such an elegant workaround.

46:08.960 --> 46:09.660
It's very elegant.

46:10.400 --> 46:13.040
But I have to push back here on a very practical level.

46:13.480 --> 46:18.380
If I am building a real-world compiler based on this logic, efficiency is everything.

46:18.380 --> 46:34.800
If every time a variable wants to move from one function to another, the computer has to pause, freeze the system, and recursively scan the variable's entire molecular structure to check if it's settled or insensitive, isn't that going to be incredibly prohibitively slow?

46:35.620 --> 46:37.820
Doesn't this bureaucracy ruin the speed of computation?

46:38.640 --> 46:43.500
That is a highly practical concern, and it's exactly what a computer scientist would ask.

46:44.120 --> 46:47.500
Flagstine anticipates this explicitly in Proposition 4.11.

46:48.380 --> 46:50.960
And the answer is a resounding no.

46:51.440 --> 46:53.020
It does not ruin efficiency.

46:53.740 --> 46:58.380
The author proves mathematically that this safety check is highly decidable.

46:59.180 --> 47:02.380
Specifically, you can traverse the term in OV time.

47:02.820 --> 47:06.320
For those who aren't software engineers, OV is big O notation.

47:06.780 --> 47:12.320
It essentially means the time it takes to check the package scales linearly with the size of the package, V.

47:12.740 --> 47:17.020
If the package has 50 pieces of data, it takes roughly 50 steps to check it.

47:17.020 --> 47:21.160
You aren't caught in some infinite loop of checking and rechecking.

47:21.980 --> 47:22.000
Exactly.

47:22.480 --> 47:24.640
It requires a single swift traversal.

47:25.200 --> 47:27.320
The computer sweeps through the structure once.

47:27.840 --> 47:30.440
It checks the spine to see if any combinators can fire.

47:31.120 --> 47:35.340
It checks the effect of permissions using very fast, simple bit mask intersections.

47:35.580 --> 47:36.420
Okay, so it's quick.

47:36.660 --> 47:37.160
Very quick.

47:37.160 --> 47:42.220
It looks for any internal brackets acting as functions to spot pending absorptions.

47:42.820 --> 47:45.040
And it scans for sensitive structural tools.

47:45.620 --> 47:46.260
It's one pass.

47:47.000 --> 47:49.800
If it passes the check, you transfer the data.

47:50.360 --> 47:52.320
If it fails, you wait.

47:53.160 --> 47:55.900
The bureaucracy is actually exceptionally optimized.

47:55.900 --> 48:03.080
I love that we are talking about bureaucracy because that perfectly bridges us into the next major conceptual leap in the paper.

48:04.040 --> 48:06.360
We have been talking about these formulas as trees.

48:06.780 --> 48:08.840
A function branches out, holds some data.

48:09.020 --> 48:11.300
Maybe that data branches out into a nested room.

48:11.660 --> 48:13.960
It's a physical branching tree of logic.

48:14.180 --> 48:14.260
Right.

48:14.360 --> 48:15.080
A tree structure.

48:15.080 --> 48:20.480
And when a tree wants to use a piece of data in two different places, it literally copies the data.

48:20.760 --> 48:22.580
It physically duplicates the branch.

48:23.000 --> 48:26.400
This is called independent copying or call-by-name evaluation.

48:26.880 --> 48:27.160
Yes.

48:27.520 --> 48:31.860
And that is how pure mathematical logic usually conceptualizes things.

48:32.540 --> 48:37.860
But in the real world of hardware and silicon, physical duplication is incredibly expensive.

48:37.860 --> 48:47.840
If a computer needs to use a massive gigabyte-sized chunk of data in two different functions, copying that entire chunk of data wastes a tremendous amount of RAM.

48:48.160 --> 48:48.400
Right.

48:48.620 --> 48:51.560
So real-world computers don't usually use pure trees.

48:51.720 --> 48:52.440
They use graphs.

48:53.100 --> 49:03.420
Instead of copying the data, the computer leaves the data in one specific spot in memory, and it just draws two different arrows, two pointers, from the two functions back to that single piece of data.

49:03.740 --> 49:04.880
They share a reference to it.

49:04.960 --> 49:06.780
This is called call-by-need evaluation.

49:06.780 --> 49:07.940
It saves a ton of memory.

49:08.640 --> 49:20.780
And in standard, universally governed computer science, independent copying, meaning trees, and reference sharing, meaning graphs, are mathematically proven to produce the exact same final result.

49:21.300 --> 49:23.920
They just get there with different levels of memory efficiency.

49:24.240 --> 49:24.480
Right.

49:24.980 --> 49:25.360
Usually.

49:25.620 --> 49:29.020
But we aren't in a universally governed system anymore.

49:29.560 --> 49:35.080
We are in a scoped universe, where your permissions depend entirely on your geographic location.

49:35.400 --> 49:35.920
Exactly.

49:35.920 --> 49:38.180
And this is where my brain started breaking.

49:38.980 --> 49:45.440
If a single, physically shared piece of data is simultaneously pointed to by two different rooms, what rules is it supposed to follow?

49:46.000 --> 49:47.820
How do scopes handle shared references?

49:48.380 --> 49:51.540
This introduces the concept of scoped term graphs.

49:52.180 --> 49:56.560
And to understand how they work, we have to define a state of harmony called coherence.

49:57.200 --> 49:59.440
Let's say you have a single piece of shared data in a graph.

49:59.820 --> 50:00.700
Node X.

50:01.020 --> 50:01.620
Node X.

50:01.620 --> 50:05.440
Because it's a shared graph, X has multiple parents pointing to it.

50:05.940 --> 50:09.220
You can trace a path from the root down to X via parent A.

50:09.800 --> 50:13.660
Or you can trace a completely different path from the root down to X via parent B.

50:13.900 --> 50:16.640
So X is a single employee in a corporation.

50:17.100 --> 50:21.020
And they report directly to two different bosses, boss A and boss B.

50:21.020 --> 50:22.160
A perfect analogy.

50:23.160 --> 50:24.920
Coherence asks a simple question.

50:26.480 --> 50:34.760
If you trace the path from the root through boss A down to employee X, what exact set of effective permissions do you accumulate?

50:34.760 --> 50:41.200
And if you trace the path through boss B down to employee X, what set of effective permissions do you accumulate?

50:42.280 --> 50:42.480
Okay.

50:42.780 --> 50:51.020
If every single possible path leading to X results in the exact same set of permissions, then node X is coherent.

50:51.980 --> 50:57.560
Meaning both boss A and boss B handed the employee the exact same corporate rulebook.

50:57.780 --> 51:00.780
They are in total agreement about what the employee is allowed to do.

51:00.780 --> 51:01.380
Exactly.

51:02.660 --> 51:07.820
And theorem 5.2 proves that if a graph is coherent, the universe remains stable.

51:08.860 --> 51:16.580
The tree semantics, where you literally copy the employee, and the graph semantics, where they share the employee, will align perfectly.

51:17.340 --> 51:18.580
The math works out the same.

51:19.300 --> 51:20.620
But what if the bosses don't agree?

51:21.140 --> 51:24.840
What if boss A is standing out in the permissive lobby and says,

51:24.840 --> 51:27.200
You are fully authorized to delete files.

51:27.820 --> 51:31.080
And boss B is standing inside a highly restricted room and says,

51:31.500 --> 51:34.060
You are strictly forbidden from ever deleting files.

51:34.700 --> 51:39.960
And employee X is just one person, sitting at one desk, receiving both instructions simultaneously.

51:40.720 --> 51:42.360
That is incoherence.

51:43.040 --> 51:49.060
The shared node X now exists in a state of superposition between two contradictory jurisdictions.

51:49.060 --> 51:53.960
And this is where the mathematical reality literally fractures.

51:54.400 --> 51:54.920
Fractures.

51:55.220 --> 52:03.060
Flyxion details this beautifully in Example 5.4, showing that when coherence fails, three entirely distinct realities emerge,

52:03.660 --> 52:08.620
depending entirely on how the computer's memory policy is programmed to resolve the conflict.

52:08.840 --> 52:14.280
I want to map these three realities out very clearly, because this is my favorite part of the entire paper.

52:14.640 --> 52:17.960
It is the ultimate bureaucratic nightmare, formalized in math.

52:17.960 --> 52:20.540
Let's set up the specific employee task.

52:21.140 --> 52:25.320
Let's say employee X is a package of data that wants to execute a delete command.

52:25.920 --> 52:28.400
It is K bracket B comma C.

52:29.100 --> 52:31.860
It wants to delete C and just become B.

52:32.200 --> 52:35.300
Okay, the task on the desk is delete C.

52:35.640 --> 52:39.600
Now, because this is the graph, we use the W combinator to share this node.

52:39.600 --> 52:48.340
The exact formula in the paper wraps employee X in two different scopes via a helper function, but the geometric result is what matters.

52:48.920 --> 52:50.020
X has two parents.

52:50.700 --> 52:53.460
Parent 1 is wrapping X in scope L1.

52:54.120 --> 52:56.740
Parent 2 is wrapping X in scope L2.

52:56.740 --> 52:57.560
Okay, got it.

52:58.220 --> 53:05.580
X exists once in the physical memory of the computer, but it is being viewed through two different architectural lenses.

53:05.920 --> 53:07.020
Let's define the permissions.

53:07.720 --> 53:09.240
The root lobby allows everything.

53:10.240 --> 53:12.700
Boss 1, scope L1 is lenient.

53:13.080 --> 53:14.520
It allows the delete key, K.

53:15.500 --> 53:18.880
But boss 2, scope L2 is strict.

53:19.440 --> 53:22.120
It explicitly forbids the delete key, K.

53:22.680 --> 53:23.800
Okay, the trap is set.

53:24.440 --> 53:25.720
Boss 1 says delete it.

53:26.080 --> 53:27.800
Boss 2 says do not delete it.

53:28.460 --> 53:29.840
Employee X is holding the file.

53:30.320 --> 53:30.920
What happens?

53:31.400 --> 53:32.980
You said there are three realities.

53:33.480 --> 53:37.300
Let's look at reality 1, the pure tree system.

53:38.020 --> 53:40.080
In this reality, we don't actually share memory.

53:40.900 --> 53:45.520
When the system sees two parents, it literally physically duplicates employee X.

53:45.980 --> 53:50.880
It builds two separate desks and puts a clone of X at each one.

53:50.880 --> 53:59.480
Okay, so we have clone 1 sitting in room L1 with boss 1, and we have clone 2 sitting in room L2 with boss 2.

53:59.860 --> 54:01.840
They are entirely independent now.

54:02.080 --> 54:02.520
Exactly.

54:03.720 --> 54:06.320
Clone 1 is in L1, which allows deletion.

54:06.840 --> 54:11.920
So clone 1 successfully executes the command, deletes C, and becomes B.

54:12.920 --> 54:16.180
Clone 2 is in L2, which forbids deletion.

54:16.180 --> 54:21.280
So clone 2 is frozen, permanently stuck as parentheses KBC.

54:21.920 --> 54:34.620
So the final resolved answer in the pure tree reality is a massive formula where one branch successfully reduced to B, and the other branch is permanently jammed as parentheses KBC.

54:34.620 --> 54:38.420
The paper gives this specific final state of name, right?

54:38.600 --> 54:40.520
It calls it normal form N2.

54:41.000 --> 54:41.220
Yes.

54:41.600 --> 54:49.740
But remember, in a pure tree system, both clones technically started out in the root lobby before they were pulled into their respective rooms.

54:50.400 --> 54:50.660
Right.

54:50.660 --> 55:03.200
Depending on the timing, depending on if they evaluated before or after crossing the boundary, clone 2 could have executed its delete command while it was still in the lobby before boss 2 restricted it.

55:03.720 --> 55:06.800
If it did that, both clones would successfully become B.

55:07.460 --> 55:10.040
The paper calls that state normal form N1.

55:10.040 --> 55:19.220
So because of the timing quirks of crossing boundaries, the tree reality actually has two entirely valid possible outcomes.

55:19.920 --> 55:24.940
N1, where both succeed, and N2, where one succeeds and one is jammed.

55:25.180 --> 55:25.640
Exactly.

55:26.320 --> 55:28.060
Keep those two valid outcomes in mind.

55:28.900 --> 55:33.760
Now let's shift to reality 2, a computer running graph policy any.

55:33.760 --> 55:44.880
This is a system that uses shared memory, and its programmed rule for resolving jurisdictional conflicts is, if any path to the shared node allows a step, execute the step.

55:45.280 --> 55:47.140
That's the chaotic startup policy.

55:47.480 --> 55:47.800
Right.

55:48.280 --> 55:52.280
If either of your bosses says yes, just go for it and ignore the other one.

55:52.540 --> 55:52.800
Right.

55:53.420 --> 55:55.340
So employee X looks at its paths.

55:56.020 --> 55:57.780
Path L1 says yes to deletion.

55:58.220 --> 55:59.340
Path L2 says no.

55:59.340 --> 56:03.920
Since any path allows it, the system authorizes the step.

56:04.580 --> 56:07.280
X executes the delete command and becomes B.

56:07.900 --> 56:08.340
But wait.

56:08.640 --> 56:14.180
Because X is shared memory in a graph, it becomes B for both parents simultaneously.

56:14.960 --> 56:21.920
Even boss 2, who explicitly forbade the deletion, looks down at the desk and suddenly sees that the file has been deleted.

56:22.480 --> 56:26.040
The strict room has been contaminated by the rules of the lenient room.

56:26.360 --> 56:26.760
Exactly.

56:26.760 --> 56:33.920
Under graph policy any, the system successfully reaches state N1, where both sides see B.

56:34.680 --> 56:38.180
But it completely, permanently misses state N2.

56:38.740 --> 56:46.280
The act of sharing memory forcefully suppressed a valid mathematical reality that independent copying would have allowed.

56:46.720 --> 56:47.120
Wow.

56:47.900 --> 56:52.500
Okay, so policy any forces a consensus by just letting the most permissive rule win.

56:53.000 --> 56:54.060
What about reality 3?

56:54.060 --> 56:57.900
Reality 3 is a computer running graph policy all.

56:58.780 --> 57:00.840
This system is highly conservative.

57:01.780 --> 57:10.360
Its rule for resolving conflicts is a step is only permitted if it is explicitly allowed on all possible access paths.

57:10.900 --> 57:13.120
Both bosses must agree or you do nothing.

57:13.400 --> 57:13.640
Right.

57:13.640 --> 57:15.660
So employee X checks.

57:16.320 --> 57:17.440
Path L1 says yes.

57:18.160 --> 57:19.180
Path L2 says no.

57:19.940 --> 57:21.660
They do not have unanimous agreement.

57:22.500 --> 57:24.160
Therefore, the step is strictly blocked.

57:24.860 --> 57:25.620
The node freezes.

57:26.260 --> 57:29.920
So employee X is permanently stuck holding KBC.

57:30.720 --> 57:31.660
It can't evaluate.

57:31.660 --> 57:37.940
Which means the final output of the formula has KBC showing up in both branches.

57:38.440 --> 57:40.420
The paper calls this state N3.

57:40.860 --> 57:41.160
Yes.

57:41.540 --> 57:43.700
And here is the absolute kicker of the whole paper.

57:44.720 --> 57:49.500
State N3 is a state that is mathematically impossible to reach in the pure tree system.

57:49.980 --> 57:51.500
It is an artificial reality.

57:51.500 --> 57:51.940
Wait.

57:52.060 --> 57:53.200
I need to wrap my head around that.

57:53.320 --> 57:55.480
Why is it impossible in the tree system?

57:56.000 --> 57:57.460
Think back to reality 1.

57:57.800 --> 57:58.280
The tree.

57:59.120 --> 58:03.500
In the tree, clone 1 is completely independent and sits alone in room L1.

58:04.200 --> 58:06.700
Room L1 explicitly allows deletion.

58:07.560 --> 58:08.720
Clone 1 has the tool.

58:09.240 --> 58:09.980
It has the permission.

58:10.560 --> 58:11.760
And nothing is stopping it.

58:12.560 --> 58:16.340
Clone 1 would never just freeze as parentheses KBC.

58:16.340 --> 58:21.080
It would always, 100% of the time, execute the step and reduce to B.

58:21.800 --> 58:22.980
Oh, I see.

58:23.420 --> 58:29.200
In the graph policy all system, the branch in room L1 stopped reducing even though it physically

58:29.200 --> 58:30.660
possessed the permission to do so.

58:30.940 --> 58:34.980
It froze purely because it was tethered to a restrictive sibling in another room?

58:35.400 --> 58:35.920
Exactly.

58:36.620 --> 58:41.860
This violates the very definition of what a normal form is supposed to be in combinatorial

58:41.860 --> 58:42.180
logic.

58:43.040 --> 58:45.780
Policy all doesn't just miss valid possibilities.

58:45.780 --> 58:51.200
It actively hallucinates a completely new, artificially deadlocked reality that could

58:51.200 --> 58:53.680
not physically exist if you just copied the data.

58:53.960 --> 58:56.680
That is just, it's a terrifying thought for a software engineer.

58:57.320 --> 58:58.560
One boss says delete this.

58:58.900 --> 59:00.460
The other boss says never delete anything.

59:00.700 --> 59:05.400
And so the shared employee just freezes forever, actively failing a task they had full

59:05.400 --> 59:07.080
legal permission to complete half the time.

59:07.620 --> 59:11.480
It proves that sharing computational memory is not the same thing as sharing authority.

59:11.800 --> 59:13.020
It's a profound demonstration.

59:13.020 --> 59:20.000
The union of contexts, policy any, allows actions that illegally contaminate restricted

59:20.000 --> 59:20.400
scopes.

59:21.280 --> 59:25.760
The intersection of contexts, policy all, creates artificial deadlocks that shouldn't

59:25.760 --> 59:26.160
exist.

59:26.560 --> 59:27.520
It's a mess either way.

59:27.940 --> 59:28.340
Exactly.

59:28.840 --> 59:34.160
The moment incoherence appears in your graph, the very fabric of how logic is evaluated splinters

59:34.160 --> 59:37.000
into three fundamentally incompatible semantic universes.

59:37.000 --> 59:42.280
And this is why Flyxion spends so much time rigorously proving the exact conditions where

59:42.280 --> 59:43.240
coherence holds.

59:43.580 --> 59:48.380
If you are building a compiler for a new programming language based on this math, you have to know

59:48.380 --> 59:52.220
with absolute certainty which of those three universes your code is living in.

59:52.300 --> 59:55.360
Otherwise, your program will yield totally unpredictable results.

59:55.360 --> 59:56.140
Indeed.

59:57.140 --> 01:00:01.620
And understanding how these permissions conflict naturally raises questions about how these

01:00:01.620 --> 01:00:06.580
permissions evolve over the entire lifespan of a running program, which takes us directly

01:00:06.580 --> 01:00:12.160
into section six of the paper, the flow of time, or what Flyxion poetically calls the

01:00:12.160 --> 01:00:13.960
descent of admissibility.

01:00:13.960 --> 01:00:14.560
Right.

01:00:14.720 --> 01:00:18.920
Because up until now, we've mostly been talking about permissions in a static snapshot.

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:23.280
We look at a frozen formula and ask, what is allowed right here, right now?

01:00:23.900 --> 01:00:25.420
But computation is a process.

01:00:25.780 --> 01:00:30.480
It is a living thing that moves forward in time, digesting itself, collapsing brackets,

01:00:30.760 --> 01:00:31.940
moving data around.

01:00:32.540 --> 01:00:37.440
How do the permissions governing a specific piece of data change as the formula digests itself

01:00:37.440 --> 01:00:37.960
over time?

01:00:38.180 --> 01:00:43.720
The paper formalizes this temporal evolution in Lemma 6.1 and Theorem 6.2.

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:47.060
And the mathematical conclusion is stark and unforgiving.

01:00:47.680 --> 01:00:52.320
As a term reduces over time, the effective permissions of any surviving data can only

01:00:52.320 --> 01:00:54.320
decrease or stay the same.

01:00:54.600 --> 01:00:56.320
They never, ever increase.

01:00:56.640 --> 01:00:59.340
It's like a logical version of entropy or gravity.

01:00:59.340 --> 01:01:02.020
You can only lose privileges as you compute.

01:01:02.180 --> 01:01:04.080
You can never organically regain them.

01:01:04.700 --> 01:01:08.240
Let's trace logically why this has to be true based on the universe we've built.

01:01:08.720 --> 01:01:12.800
Think about a core step like executing a copy, a delete, or a reorder.

01:01:12.800 --> 01:01:15.060
What do those tools actually do?

01:01:15.660 --> 01:01:16.680
They just rearrange the data.

01:01:17.120 --> 01:01:17.400
Exactly.

01:01:17.760 --> 01:01:20.700
They just rearrange the data that is sitting directly in front of them.

01:01:21.120 --> 01:01:23.160
They do not push data through any doors.

01:01:23.380 --> 01:01:24.400
They don't cross boundaries.

01:01:24.800 --> 01:01:28.520
So the physical chain of brackets surrounding the data remains exactly the same.

01:01:29.120 --> 01:01:31.220
No boundary crossed means no permission change.

01:01:31.740 --> 01:01:31.960
Okay.

01:01:32.080 --> 01:01:34.340
So the core tools don't change permissions.

01:01:34.840 --> 01:01:36.520
What about the boundary rules?

01:01:36.780 --> 01:01:38.460
The things that actually move data?

01:01:38.460 --> 01:01:44.260
The boundary rules are SCR, which just wraps a piece of data in a brand new bracket, and

01:01:44.260 --> 01:01:47.660
ABS, which we discussed, pulling data into an existing bracket.

01:01:48.360 --> 01:01:51.240
What is the geometric result of both of those actions?

01:01:51.800 --> 01:01:56.360
In both cases, you are adding a brand new label to the chain of scopes enclosing the data.

01:01:56.360 --> 01:02:01.660
And as we established an hour ago, whenever you add a new label to a chain, you have to

01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:06.540
calculate the new effective permission by intersecting the old rules with the new rules.

01:02:06.920 --> 01:02:10.700
And an intersection in set theory can only ever shrink a set.

01:02:10.900 --> 01:02:13.080
It can never add a new element that wasn't there before.

01:02:13.080 --> 01:02:13.900
Precisely.

01:02:14.440 --> 01:02:18.840
If you didn't have the copy power before you walked through a door, intersecting your

01:02:18.840 --> 01:02:23.880
rules with the new room's rules is never, mathematically, going to magically grant you

01:02:23.880 --> 01:02:24.660
the copy power.

01:02:25.160 --> 01:02:26.780
You can only lose what you have.

01:02:27.360 --> 01:02:31.500
The paper uses two terms here that I think are really helpful for programmers, static

01:02:31.500 --> 01:02:33.940
classification versus dynamic classification.

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:35.220
Let's define those.

01:02:35.840 --> 01:02:40.320
Static classification is what a compiler does when it just looks at the raw code before running

01:02:40.320 --> 01:02:40.540
it.

01:02:40.540 --> 01:02:45.380
It looks at the physical layout of the nested brackets and calculates the absolute maximum

01:02:45.380 --> 01:02:49.260
set of permissions that any part of the formula could ever possibly use.

01:02:50.160 --> 01:02:53.860
But Flitscham points out that this static view is just a bound.

01:02:54.160 --> 01:02:54.740
It's a ceiling.

01:02:54.960 --> 01:02:55.980
It is not a destiny.

01:02:56.340 --> 01:02:58.320
What do you mean by not a destiny?

01:02:58.560 --> 01:03:00.720
If the code says a bracket is there, isn't it there?

01:03:01.340 --> 01:03:04.800
Let's look at an example from the paper, Proposition 6.3.

01:03:04.800 --> 01:03:11.800
Suppose you have a formula, a function K, sitting inside a restricted room, L, waiting to absorb

01:03:11.800 --> 01:03:14.240
a piece of data, B, from the lobby.

01:03:15.180 --> 01:03:19.300
Let's say the root lobby allows K, but room L strictly forbids K.

01:03:20.160 --> 01:03:25.620
Okay, so statically, if I am just reading the code on the page, I see the K tool physically

01:03:25.620 --> 01:03:30.340
sitting inside room L. Therefore, I statically conclude, ah, this tool is blocked.

01:03:30.340 --> 01:03:31.300
It will never fire.

01:03:32.200 --> 01:03:34.500
But watch what happens dynamically when you press run.

01:03:35.500 --> 01:03:41.900
The function inside room L absorbs B. So now you have paren K, A, B sitting inside room

01:03:41.900 --> 01:03:47.800
L. The K tool has its arguments, but it's inside a restricted room, so it freezes.

01:03:48.800 --> 01:03:50.180
Your static prediction was correct.

01:03:50.280 --> 01:03:51.200
The tool didn't fire.

01:03:51.200 --> 01:03:53.580
Okay, but what's the alternative?

01:03:54.180 --> 01:03:57.160
The alternative is the airport security example we did earlier.

01:03:58.180 --> 01:04:04.220
What if the initial formula was A inside room L and paren KBC waiting out in the lobby?

01:04:05.360 --> 01:04:10.380
Statically, a compiler might scan that code, see that paren KBC is eventually going to be

01:04:10.380 --> 01:04:16.580
absorbed into room L, and say, well, room L forbids K, so this system will ultimately never

01:04:16.580 --> 01:04:17.520
use the K rule.

01:04:17.520 --> 01:04:24.700
But, if we dynamically execute the code and we evaluate paren KBC before it gets absorbed,

01:04:25.240 --> 01:04:27.420
it is sitting in the permissive lobby.

01:04:27.800 --> 01:04:29.420
It fires, it deletes C.

01:04:29.660 --> 01:04:30.040
Exactly.

01:04:30.400 --> 01:04:36.200
Dynamically, depending purely on the execution order, the system did use the K rule, even though

01:04:36.200 --> 01:04:39.440
a static scan of the final destination would imply it couldn't.

01:04:39.940 --> 01:04:43.820
The scope tree on the page only provides a boundary on what is possible.

01:04:43.820 --> 01:04:49.620
The dynamic classification, when it actually unfolds chronologically when the code runs,

01:04:50.120 --> 01:04:54.520
is the only true measure of what structural powers a term will actually utilize.

01:04:54.880 --> 01:04:59.680
So, you can't just scan the code and definitively know what logical rules it obeys.

01:04:59.860 --> 01:05:05.360
You have to actually run it or map out every single possible execution path, which is a huge

01:05:05.360 --> 01:05:09.540
headache for static analysis, but a beautiful reality of dynamic systems.

01:05:09.840 --> 01:05:10.420
It really is.

01:05:10.420 --> 01:05:15.280
This brings us to the final, massive conceptual zoom out of the paper.

01:05:15.640 --> 01:05:19.960
We've spent this whole time deep in the weeds of specific tools, copy, delete, reorder.

01:05:20.480 --> 01:05:25.800
But in Section 7, Flyxion steps back and asks, how does this concept of geographic permissions

01:05:25.800 --> 01:05:29.040
apply to abstract transition systems at large?

01:05:29.040 --> 01:05:29.540
Yes.

01:05:29.760 --> 01:05:34.820
He zooms out from the specific combinators and builds an abstract mathematical framework.

01:05:35.140 --> 01:05:41.140
He defines a system purely in terms of configurations, transitions, and admissibility rules.

01:05:41.800 --> 01:05:46.560
It's like stepping out of our specific corporate office building and looking at the very concept

01:05:46.560 --> 01:05:48.540
of architecture and physics themselves.

01:05:48.540 --> 01:05:49.020
Wow.

01:05:49.480 --> 01:05:55.440
And the big provocative question he asks in this section is, what happens if we take this complex,

01:05:55.580 --> 01:05:59.820
highly restricted, scoped universe, and we just erase all the boundaries?

01:06:00.320 --> 01:06:02.840
What if we tear down all the walls?

01:06:03.060 --> 01:06:04.560
This is the ultimate what if.

01:06:04.720 --> 01:06:09.620
If we erase the brackets, if we tear down the walls, does the system just seamlessly revert back

01:06:09.620 --> 01:06:11.460
to normal, unrestricted logic?

01:06:11.460 --> 01:06:18.000
To answer that, the paper analyzes this process, the abstraction of erasing boundaries, using

01:06:18.000 --> 01:06:23.040
three formal mathematical properties, preservation, reflection, and lifting.

01:06:23.680 --> 01:06:28.640
Let's break them down one by one because they tell a fascinating story about the physical

01:06:28.640 --> 01:06:29.460
nature of logic.

01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:31.500
First, preservation.

01:06:32.540 --> 01:06:36.080
Does erasing the boundaries preserve the validity of the steps you took?

01:06:36.780 --> 01:06:38.060
Let me think about that.

01:06:38.060 --> 01:06:42.440
If a mathematical step was legally allowed when there were strict rules, locked doors,

01:06:42.560 --> 01:06:47.020
and banned tools everywhere, it should definitely still be allowed when you remove all the doors

01:06:47.020 --> 01:06:49.500
and make everything universally legal, right?

01:06:50.200 --> 01:06:50.680
Yes.

01:06:51.420 --> 01:06:55.000
Proposition 7.1 proves that preservation holds unconditionally.

01:06:55.840 --> 01:07:00.940
Every single step that was admissible in the strict, scoped system remains a perfectly valid

01:07:00.940 --> 01:07:03.600
step in the unscoped, boundary-free world.

01:07:03.600 --> 01:07:08.740
The abstract semantics act as an over-approximation of the concrete ones.

01:07:09.220 --> 01:07:12.320
Everything that was possible before is still possible now.

01:07:12.560 --> 01:07:13.120
Okay.

01:07:13.260 --> 01:07:15.060
That makes perfect intuitive sense.

01:07:15.560 --> 01:07:17.600
What about the second property, reflection?

01:07:18.400 --> 01:07:21.200
Reflection asks the exact reverse question.

01:07:21.740 --> 01:07:26.900
If I observe a sequence of valid steps happening in the abstract, boundary-free world,

01:07:26.900 --> 01:07:33.860
can I safely assume that those exact same steps would also be valid back in the strict, scoped world?

01:07:34.620 --> 01:07:35.900
Well, definitely not.

01:07:36.220 --> 01:07:40.040
We literally just spent an hour proving that scopes exist to block things.

01:07:40.460 --> 01:07:44.800
If I execute a delete command in the boundary-free world, it works perfectly.

01:07:45.360 --> 01:07:48.080
But if I try to map that action back into the scoped world,

01:07:48.320 --> 01:07:53.240
I might discover that the delete command was actually sitting inside a restricted VIP room

01:07:53.240 --> 01:07:54.740
that explicitly banned it.

01:07:54.740 --> 01:07:55.820
Precisely.

01:07:56.420 --> 01:08:00.740
Proposition 7.2 formally proves that reflection fails entirely.

01:08:01.380 --> 01:08:08.220
It fails precisely because a scope has the power to withdraw a permission that the abstract root naturally grants.

01:08:08.840 --> 01:08:14.360
You cannot map the freedom of a boundary-free world back onto the strictness of a concrete walled world.

01:08:14.360 --> 01:08:21.980
Okay, so preservation works, strict rules map to free rules, but reflection fails, free rules do not map to strict rules.

01:08:22.480 --> 01:08:23.980
What is the third property?

01:08:24.300 --> 01:08:25.220
What is lifting?

01:08:26.180 --> 01:08:28.720
Lifting asks a slightly more nuanced question.

01:08:29.040 --> 01:08:29.480
It asks,

01:08:30.120 --> 01:08:32.880
For every valid move that happens in the boundary-free world,

01:08:33.360 --> 01:08:38.680
is there some corresponding valid move you can make in the scoped world right now to keep pace?

01:08:38.680 --> 01:08:43.760
Hmm. If reflection fails because you might lack the permission to do something,

01:08:44.280 --> 01:08:46.220
lifting must fail for the exact same reason, right?

01:08:46.400 --> 01:08:51.220
You try to make a move, but the scoped world says permission denied, so you can't lift the action.

01:08:51.780 --> 01:08:54.480
Actually, this is where the paper delivers a brilliant twist.

01:08:55.100 --> 01:08:59.440
The author proves that lifting fails for a completely fundamentally independent reason.

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:04.760
It fails not just because of a loss of permission, but because of a loss of exposure.

01:09:04.760 --> 01:09:10.220
Loss of exposure. Okay, I think I have an analogy for this. Tell me if this tracks with the math.

01:09:10.340 --> 01:09:10.740
Go for it.

01:09:11.200 --> 01:09:14.240
Imagine a zoo. You have a lion, and right next to it you have a zebra.

01:09:14.580 --> 01:09:17.060
But between them is a massive, thick, concrete wall.

01:09:17.540 --> 01:09:20.020
That wall is our boundary, our scope bracket.

01:09:20.580 --> 01:09:23.700
Now, in the scoped world, the lion cannot eat the zebra. Why?

01:09:24.120 --> 01:09:27.660
It's not because eating zebras is against the rules or lacks permission.

01:09:28.100 --> 01:09:32.020
The lion can't eat the zebra because the wall physically interrupts the interaction.

01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:33.460
They literally cannot touch.

01:09:33.460 --> 01:09:37.340
That is an absolutely perfect visualization of the syntax tree.

01:09:38.100 --> 01:09:43.320
The bracket literally sits on the spine of the formula, wedged directly between the function and the argument.

01:09:43.980 --> 01:09:48.260
In the paper's terminology, the red X, the potential interaction is not exposed.

01:09:49.080 --> 01:09:57.340
Let's say you have the formula, the identity combinator I sitting inside bracket L trying to interact with data A outside the bracket.

01:09:57.900 --> 01:10:00.920
The bracket L is a physical wedge between I and A.

01:10:00.920 --> 01:10:05.400
Right. So, in the scoped world, the lion just sits there.

01:10:05.900 --> 01:10:08.760
The interaction doesn't even exist geometrically.

01:10:09.580 --> 01:10:16.140
